  "narrative": {
    "text": "**Business & market dynamics:** Core commercial-vehicle battery and energy storage operations face severe demand headwinds, evidenced by an inventory impairment charge and declining sales. A joint venture with Oshkosh and a planned Clarksville, Tennessee factory ramp, supported by IRA tax credits, offer a potential pivot, but near-term momentum is weak and an auditor’s going-concern warning underscores existential risk. Geopolitical tensions and competitive pressures further cloud the outlook.\n\n**Management strategy and execution:** Leadership is attempting a reset through the Oshkosh partnership and factory activation, yet a recent chief accounting officer departure adds governance uncertainty. A large earnings miss and inventory write-down have eroded credibility, and the market is pricing in high execution risk. The Clarksville ramp now represents a make‑or‑break test of whether management can steer the company away from financial distress and restore investor confidence.",
